<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<configuration>

  <!--
    Repository-local NuGet configuration.

    WHY THIS FILE EXISTS
    This machine has three feeds configured globally (nuget.org, an internal SBC
    feed, and the Visual Studio offline folder). With more than one feed and no
    mapping, NuGet may resolve a package name from ANY of them — which is the
    dependency-confusion attack: publish a package to a public feed using the same
    name as someone's internal package, and their restore may pull yours instead.

    Enabling Central Package Management turns that ambiguity into error NU1507
    rather than a warning, which is why the first build failed.

    WHAT THIS FILE DOES
    1. <clear /> discards feeds inherited from the machine-level config, so a build
       here depends only on what is declared below. A teammate or CI agent without
       the SBC feed gets an identical restore.
    2. <packageSourceMapping> states explicitly which feed may supply which package
       IDs. Every package resolves from nuget.org and nowhere else.

    ADDING AN INTERNAL PACKAGE LATER
    Declare the feed and map only its own ID prefix — never map "*" to two sources:

      <packageSources>
        <add key="sbc" value="https://sbcnuget.sbcsdaia.com/nuget" />
      </packageSources>

      <packageSourceMapping>
        <packageSource key="sbc">
          <package pattern="Sbc.*" />
        </packageSource>
      </packageSourceMapping>

    Credentials for a private feed belong in the USER-level NuGet config or in a CI
    secret — never in this file, which is committed.
  -->

  <packageSources>
    <clear />
    <add key="nuget.org" value="https://api.nuget.org/v3/index.json" protocolVersion="3" />
  </packageSources>

  <packageSourceMapping>
    <packageSource key="nuget.org">
      <package pattern="*" />
    </packageSource>
  </packageSourceMapping>

</configuration>
